It is well known that the area ratio of two triangles with a common base is equal to the ratio of the areas of the triangles that are similar to these two triangles. Let's use mass points to determine the ratios of the segments involved. Assign masses to the vertices $A$, $B$, and $C$ as follows: [asy]
size(6cm);defaultpen(fontsize(11));

pair b =(0,0), c=(10,0),a=(4,6);
pair d, e, f;
d=round(\ang{theta}/2);
e=(a+b)/3;
f=(a+d)/4;
draw(a--b--c--cycle--d--e--f);

label("$A$", a, N);
label("$B$", b, SW);
label("$C$", c, SE);
label("$D$", d, S);
label("$E$", e, NE);
label("$F$", f, SW);
[/asy]$\text{mass } A=1,\text{mass } B=2,\text{mass } C=1,\text{mass D}=3,\text{mass of} E=3,\text{mass of} F=6,$
The reciprocals of the masses give the ratios of the segments: $\text{AR:RD}=3:6=1:2,\text{BR:RD}=6:3=2:1,\text{AR:RE}=3:6=1:2.$
Because point $F$ lies on segment $\overline{AD}$, it follows that $\text{AF:FD}=3:1.$
Thus, \begin{align*}17/[DEF]&=(AE)(AF)/(AC)(AD)\\  &=\left(\tfrac{1}{3}\right)\left(\tfrac{3}{4}\right)^2\\
&=\tfrac{3}{16} \cdot [ABC]\end{align*}Since $\tfrac{3}{16}[ABC]=17$, the area of $\triangle ABC$ is $\tfrac{16}{3} \cdot 17 = \boxed{85}.$</think> <answer>85</answer>

